# Pre-Launch Engineering Policy — Single Implementation Rule

**Status:** PRODUCT ARCHITECTURE DIRECTIVE · Default until Catalyst One official production Go-Live  
**Authority:** Product Architecture  
**Does not modify:** ADR-018 (frozen as certified)

## Principle

Until official production Go-Live, Catalyst One optimises for a **clean canonical architecture**, not long-lived dual-path backward compatibility.

For every business capability there shall be **ONLY ONE active implementation**.

## When Product Architecture says “Replace”

The replacement becomes the **only** active implementation of that capability.

The superseded implementation must **immediately** stop participating in:

- Navigation  
- Routing  
- User Journey  
- Workflow  
- Business Logic  

Then retire or remove it in a controlled manner (code deletion / dead-path quarantine after Replacement Certification).

## Why (BAT lesson)

Parallel active implementations of the same capability inflate BAT investigation cost (“which path handled this?”). Pre-launch, that cost outweighs legacy dual-run value.

## Replacement Certification (mandatory)

Every replacement sprint is incomplete until Product Owner accepts a **Replacement Certification** that confirms:

1. Which implementation is now **canonical**  
2. Which legacy implementation has been **retired**  
3. Which **routes** were removed or redirected  
4. Which **navigation** entries were removed  
5. Which **workflow** references were updated  
6. That **only one** active implementation of that capability remains  

Template: `docs/governance/REPLACEMENT-CERTIFICATION-TEMPLATE.md`

## Business Capability Ownership

Every major business capability shall have: one canonical business identity · one canonical route · one primary workspace · one authoritative data owner · one active implementation (until Go-Live).

See `.cursor/rules/business-capability-ownership.mdc` · ADR-019 §3A.

## Legacy Retirement Impact (mandatory in Assessments & ADRs)

Every Product Architecture Assessment and ADR that proposes a **Replace** shall include a section titled **Legacy Retirement Impact** documenting:

- Business capability being replaced  
- Current vs proposed canonical implementation  
- Legacy routes, navigation, and components/services to retire  
- Expected Replacement Certification deliverables  
- Confirmation that only **one** active implementation will remain after implementation  

Reference example: `docs/adr/ADR-019-co-arch-004-deal-workspace-identity.md` §11.

## Scope boundary

- **Applies:** All future Product Architecture replacement programmes until Go-Live.  
- **Does not reopen:** Certified ADRs (including ADR-018) unless a new ADR explicitly supersedes them.  
- **Post–Go-Live:** Compatibility strategy may be reconsidered under a separate Production Continuity policy.

## Agent behaviour

When implementing a Product Architecture “Replace” decision:

1. Wire **only** the canonical path into nav, routes, journey, and business logic.  
2. Do **not** leave the legacy path as an alternate active journey.  
3. Do **not** mark the sprint complete without Replacement Certification artefacts.  
4. Prefer controlled retirement over silent dual-run “just in case.”
